# aka-claude-tools.config — opt-in, environment-specific policy for the guard hooks
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# This file tunes two aka-claude-tools hooks to YOUR environment. It ships with
# every value empty, so the hooks are no-ops until you set something here — the
# kit never assumes anything about your org, network, or tooling.
#
#   WHAT THIS IS   — policy and patterns: what to redact, what to steer away from.
#   WHAT IT IS NOT — a place for secrets. Nothing here is a credential. These are
#                    the patterns that keep credentials and internal details from
#                    leaking — never the secrets themselves.
#
# How it's loaded (first hit wins):
#   1. $CLAUDETOOLS_CONFIG                          explicit override
#   2. $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/aka-claude-tools.config   your active profile
#   3. ~/.claude/aka-claude-tools.config            default profile
#
# It's a shell file (`KEY="value"`, sourced by bash) and is restored automatically
# across `install.sh` upgrades — set it once. To use it:
#   cp aka-claude-tools.config.example "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/aka-claude-tools.config"
# then uncomment what you need below.


# ── leak-guard.sh — keep internal details from leaving the building ─
# Threat model: an agent pastes an internal hostname, IP, username, absolute home
# path, or unreleased codename into a *web search/fetch* query or an *outbound*
# shell command (curl/wget/nc/…), quietly handing your topology to a third party.
# This is a per-environment denylist of identifiers that must never egress; a hit
# blocks the query/command before it leaves.
#
# CT_EGRESS_PATTERNS — ONE regex, pipe-separated alternatives. Substring match,
# case-sensitive; escape any literal dots. It is matched by BOTH the web guard
# (grep -E) and the Bash guard (JavaScript), so it MUST use the PORTABLE SUBSET:
# character classes like [0-9] / [A-Za-z] — NOT \d / \w / \s / \b, NO POSIX classes
# [[:alpha:]], NO lookaround (?…). install.sh REJECTS non-portable constructs (they
# behave differently in the two engines and would leak on one surface). The value is
# compiled into an inert JSON sidecar at install — edit it, then re-run ./install.sh
# to apply it. Categories to mix:
#
#   internal / tailnet domains   acme\.internal | [a-z0-9-]+\.ts\.net
#   RFC1918 + CGNAT ranges       10\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+ | 192\.168\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+
#                                100\.(6[4-9]|[7-9][0-9]|1[01][0-9]|12[0-7])\.   # tailscale 100.64/10
#   VCS / SSO usernames          acme-vcs-bot | jdoe@acme
#   absolute home paths          /Users/jdoe/ | /home/jdoe/
#   unreleased codenames         project-bluejay | falcon-rewrite
#
# Example (one line — combine the categories you care about):
#   CT_EGRESS_PATTERNS='acme\.internal|[a-z0-9-]+\.ts\.net|10\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+|/Users/jdoe/|project-bluejay'
CT_EGRESS_PATTERNS=""


# ── harness-pointer.sh — steer the agent to the right tool for THIS environment ─
# When an agent reaches for a command that's wrong here — not installed, not
# authenticated, or simply not how your team works — intercept it and hand back
# the correct approach, instead of letting it fail confusingly and thrash through
# retries. GUIDANCE, not a security boundary: it matches only the first word, so
# `sudo <cmd>` and pipes slip past — real restrictions belong in
# settings.json `permissions.deny`.
#
# CT_BLOCKED_CMDS — ERE alternation of command names (first word) to intercept.
# CT_BLOCKED_HINT — the message returned to the agent on a match. Make it
#                   ACTIONABLE: say what to do instead, not just "no".
#
# Example — GitHub over SSH, no API token on the box (a least-privilege git
# posture: auth is scoped to git transport only, and there's no broad gh token to
# steal or pivot through):
#   CT_BLOCKED_CMDS="gh"
#   CT_BLOCKED_HINT="gh isn't authenticated here — this environment uses SSH to GitHub. Use git over SSH for clone/fetch/push, and open PRs from the branch's compare page: https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/compare/main...<branch>?expand=1 (derive <owner>/<repo> from 'git remote get-url origin')."
#
# Example — self-hosted VCS, the GitHub CLI simply doesn't apply:
#   CT_BLOCKED_CMDS="gh"
#   CT_BLOCKED_HINT="Use plain git — this team's remote is self-hosted, not GitHub."
#
# Example — keep agents off production tooling, push them to a guarded wrapper:
#   CT_BLOCKED_CMDS="kubectl|helm"
#   CT_BLOCKED_HINT="Don't touch the cluster directly — use ./scripts/deploy (it pins the context and requires an approval)."
#
# Example — enforce a toolchain choice:
#   CT_BLOCKED_CMDS="npm|npx|yarn"
#   CT_BLOCKED_HINT="This repo standardises on bun — use bun / bunx instead."
CT_BLOCKED_CMDS=""
CT_BLOCKED_HINT="This command isn't the right tool in this environment (aka-claude-tools harness-pointer) — see aka-claude-tools.config for the intended approach."


# ── mcp-guard.ts — MCP server allow/deny policy ─
# Threat model: an auto-loaded or third-party MCP server you didn't mean to grant tool
# access to. This policy is checked BEFORE the secret scan, on every mcp__* tool call.
#
# CT_MCP_ALLOW / CT_MCP_DENY — comma-separated MCP SERVER NAMES (not tool names): the
# segment right after "mcp__" in the tool name (mcp__<server>__<tool>). Matched
# case-insensitively. No spaces around commas, no leading/trailing/doubled commas
# (install.sh rejects those). CT_MCP_DENY is checked first: a server on it is always
# blocked. If CT_MCP_ALLOW is non-empty, every server NOT on it is blocked too (an empty
# CT_MCP_ALLOW means "no allow-list restriction", not "deny everything"). Both are
# compiled into an install-time sidecar (hooks/lib/mcp-policy.json); edit here, then
# re-run ./install.sh to apply.
#
# Example — allow only two servers, and explicitly deny a third even if it later
# appears on the allow list by mistake:
#   CT_MCP_ALLOW="searxng,hiring-data"
#   CT_MCP_DENY="some-untrusted-server"
CT_MCP_ALLOW=""
CT_MCP_DENY=""


# ── command-guard.ts — trusted bootstrap allowlist ─
# Threat model: legitimate installer scripts (rustup, nvm, …) are conventionally piped
# into a shell (`curl … | bash`), which is indistinguishable in shape from a malicious
# pipe-to-shell. Off by default — command-guard blocks ALL curl|bash forms until you
# name specific installer URL prefixes to trust.
#
# CT_TRUSTED_BOOTSTRAP_URLS — a SPACE-separated list of "https://host/path/" prefixes
# (note the required trailing slash — a prefix, never a specific file). Only an EXACT
# command shape is exempted, and only when it matches: `curl <allowed flags> <https url
# under one of these prefixes> | bash` or `| sh` — nothing else on the command line, no
# other pipe segment, no other positional argument. Allowed curl flags are exactly
# -f/-s/-S in any combination (and their long forms --fail/--silent/--show-error), plus
# --tlsv1.2 and --proto=https; anything else (including the common -L to follow
# redirects) falls outside the exemption and still blocks. The URL itself must be
# https, with no userinfo, port, query string, or fragment, and its path may not
# contain "." or ".." segments.
#
# Example:
#   CT_TRUSTED_BOOTSTRAP_URLS="https://sh.rustup.rs/ https://get.example.dev/install/"
#
# Residual risks — read before relying on this as a security boundary:
#   - curl itself reads ~/.curlrc (and $CURL_HOME/.curlrc) by default, which can inject
#     flags — including a redirect follow (-L / --location) — that never appear in the
#     command text this guard inspects. A permissive .curlrc can widen what an allowed
#     URL actually fetches and executes, invisibly to this allowlist.
#   - The sidecar's staleness check only detects that aka-claude-tools.config changed
#     since the sidecar was compiled (by comparing a hash of the config file). It does
#     NOT verify that the sidecar's own contents still match what that config would
#     compile to; a stale sidecar still applies its (possibly out-of-date) rules, with
#     a warning, rather than being disabled.
#   - A pathPrefix of exactly "/" matches every path on that host — it allowlists the
#     WHOLE host, not just an install-script directory. Scope pathPrefix as narrowly as
#     the installer's real URL layout allows.
CT_TRUSTED_BOOTSTRAP_URLS=""
